19.2 Database rules

- Use primary keys and appropriate foreign keys.
- Define indexes based on actual query patterns.
- Use unique constraints for identities and idempotency.
- Use explicit status fields and timestamps.
- Use UTC timestamps internally.
- Use integer minor units for monetary values.
- Store ISO currency codes.
- Avoid floating-point money calculations.
- Apply ownership checks to user-owned records.
- Define retention and deletion behavior.
- Keep sensitive data separate where appropriate.
- Avoid unnecessary personal information.
- Use transactions where supported and necessary.
- Define concurrency and consistency behavior.
- Keep audit history for sensitive operations.

19.3 Migration governance

- Inspect existing migrations before creating new ones.
- Never modify an already-applied production migration casually.
- Prefer additive migrations.
- Never drop production tables or columns without an approved data migration plan.
- Preserve existing user data.
- Test migrations against a representative database snapshot.
- Document rollback or forward-recovery procedures.
- Verify foreign keys and indexes.
- Record migration execution evidence.
- Never claim a migration is production-safe based only on successful local generation.

